  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## The Event
    
    The Pta∴extinct was a geothermal-arcane breach at Sleek☽ Caemomot, a Nightshelf deep-mining site, in which ancient power bled upward through fractured ice and crystallized into a permanent anomalous structure. It killed seventeen miners and remains an active source of corruption in the northern tunnel network.
    
    The sequence, as best reconstructed: the deep volcanic vents beneath Sleek☽ Caemomot began producing low-frequency vibrations strong enough to cause physical discomfort. The ice above the vents destabilized. A fissure opened — narrow at first, emitting light consistent with neither aurora-source nor fire-core radiation. Accounts state the light poured upward for three days, though I note that this duration appears in Nightshelf pressure-song tradition with a frequency that suggests convention rather than measurement. Miners who approached the fissure edges reported somatic impressions of ancestral memory — sensations predating their own lifetimes. The accumulated energy crystallized into **Crypt-seal☽ Teneecloris~**, a structure of reportedly impossible geometry, composed of fused light and stone. It persists. It hums on certain nights. Its influence extends through the substrate beneath the surrounding tunnels.
    
    Seventeen miners did not return from the initial breach. The figure is consistent across all accounts I have located, which is unusual enough to note — Nightshelf casualty records from this period rarely achieve such unanimity.
    
    ## Ongoing Effects
    
    The Pta∴extinct opened something that has not closed. Subsequent structural damage — orca shore-breaker impacts on the surface ice, tunnel collapses, the general degradation of war traffic — has widened the breach network rather than sealing it. Corruption seeps have spread through the northern passages. Where crystallized fissures run near the surface, whisper-echoes carry fragments resembling tactical intelligence; some of it proves accurate, which is precisely what makes it dangerous.
    
    Crypt-seal☽ Teneecloris~'s influence produces crystallized floor surfaces over void spaces that pulse with light from below and can collapse under a traveler's weight. Prolonged exposure to corrupted zones causes cold-burn discoloration. Left uncleansed, subjects begin comprehending the whispers. The Nightshelf term for those so affected — the **Touched** — is clinically precise: functional but changed, responsive to presences others cannot perceive. The chronicle account of Augur's supply run through the affected tunnels during the Frozen Peace confirms these effects remain active, and that the corruption has been incorporated into operational planning rather than eliminated.
    
    ## Commemoration
    
    The Nightshelf response to the Pta∴extinct has been characteristically exhaustive. Memorial carvings were made into at least five cavern walls — **Vestigium∴moria**, **Iudicium∴culum**, **Echo∴eliquiae**, **Umbra∴go**, and others — each attempting a distinct articulation of the event. The names of the seventeen dead are spoken annually at the **Foedus∴sper** festival, a civic ceremony at which all commerce halts until the fallen of the Pta∴extinct, the Silent War, and the Cavern Fall have been recited. Absence is noted. The event is further held within the commemorative frameworks of **Foedus∴silenti**, **Foedus∴vincu**, **Pactum∴silenti**, **Mandatum∴ccord**, and **Ritus∴um** — nine formal instruments of remembrance for a single disaster.
    
    Foreman Kaela still keeps the ice-pick of Tormin, one of the seventeen, hung above her work-station. She has never sharpened it. Nine laws of remembrance, and still the truest memorial is a tool no one uses.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771587901780_0", anchor: "nine formal instruments of remembrance for a single disaster", text: "Nine. Including the Foedus∴vincu — the Nightshelf's de facto constitution — and Ritus∴um, whose seal I have questioned three times as unfit for authentication. The Nightshelf response to catastrophic grief is to legislate it into permanence, not to heal but to ensure the wound remains load-bearing. How extraordinary that this works. How extraordinary that it has to.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771587901780_1", anchor: "The figure is consistent across all accounts I have located, which is unusual enough to note", text: "Mining guilds kept shift rosters. The unanimity is not mysterious — it is payroll. Sleek☽ Caemomot's crew manifest would have been filed with the ☽'ofund' Guild. I flagged the consistency too admiringly. This is simply the one category of Nightshelf record that corruption has not yet eaten.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587901780_2", anchor: "some of it proves accurate, which is precisely what makes it dangerous", text: "Qeihong's whispers guide her calculations. Noctisum~'s information chain runs through the taint. Now the northern tunnels offer tactical intelligence to anyone who lingers. The corruption's most effective vector is not madness — it is usefulness.", type: :commentary, display: :full
